{"title":"Gradian","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Introduction\">Introduction</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gradian&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Introduction\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A <b>gradian</b> translates into 1/400 of a full circle. It is also known as a grade or a grad. The gradian originated in France, with the introduction of the metric system, along with measurements like the centigrade. \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Conversion\">Conversion</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Gradian&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Conversion\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>0° = 0 grade ,\n90° = 100 grade ,\n180° = 200 grade ,\n270° = 300 grade ,\n360° = 400 grade .\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>One advantage of this, as shown above, is that it makes for extremely easy mental computation of right angles and other common <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Angle\" title=\"Angle\">angle</a> measures, and is useful in engineering and architecture.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Gradian&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017202217\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.008 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.012 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 11/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 262/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip re